# Controllers design

Controllers holds the Fearless manager: a desktop program that keeps a set of project folders, compiles those projects, runs the mains of those projects, and connects those projects to the desktop and to Eclipse.
Next to the manager, and independent from the manager, Controllers also holds:
- `fearlessPluginProject`: the Eclipse plugin showing the projects of a manager inside Eclipse, with content assist (see EclipseDesign).
- `agentTools`: a library driving the real pointer and keyboard, for GUI tests of any program.
- `benchmarks`: speed measures of Fearless programs against the runtime.

The manager is an island: the manager behaves the same on every system.
When a part of the system (a tray, a dialog, a desktop shell) would make the manager behave differently than this design, the manager does not use that part that way.
When the system does not offer what the manager needs (a display, a file system feature the manager relies on, at most one identity of the Fearless family on the system (see Refusals in ReconciliatorDesign), or each Fearless extension (see File associations)), or the manager folder exists and does not hold what the manager wrote there, the manager stops with a 'fatal error' saying so.
This is the only concept of 'fatal error'.

A 'fatal error' ends the manager, in this order:
-A dialog shows the error and lists the mains and the compilations that are currently running. Nothing below happens until the dialog has been closed. With no display, the error goes to the standard error of the manager program instead, and no step below waits for a dialog.
-All the running mains and compilations are terminated. The manager waits until every main and compilation has terminated.
-The manager deletes the manager folder.
-The manager removes the setup of every identity of the Fearless family (`.eradicateAll`, see ReconciliatorDesign).

Everything the manager is asked to do either happens or is refused with an error naming what was refused and the reason of the refusal.

The algorithms of this design specify the behaviour, not the implementation.

## Program and manager folder

The program folder of a manager is `fearlessManaged<version>`, for example `fearlessManaged0_001`.
On macOS the program folder is the application `fearlessManaged<version>.app`.
The 'launcher' of the manager is the executable file of the program folder that started the manager program; the program folder holds two launchers (see The data in ReconciliatorDesign).
The manager folder of a manager is `fearless<version>`, next to the program folder, on every system. The manager folder holds everything the manager remembers; deleting the manager folder makes the manager start from nothing.
A manager folder that can not be created is a 'fatal error' naming the manager folder.

## Starting the 'single' manager
A 'manager process' is an OS process running the manager program. Several manager processes can be alive at the same time, and at most one of them is the 'single' manager: the manager process that handles the messages.

The manager program takes:
- zero arguments: the message is `Show`;
- one argument starting with `FearlessCommand<Verb>`, a `call` (see Requests and messages): the message is the verb `<Verb>` with the arguments of the verb and the `port` that follow in the `call`;
- one argument not starting with `FearlessCommand`: the path of a file or a folder, as the system passes the path when the file is opened (for example by a double click); the messages are `Register` with that path, then `Show`, for a folder or a `.fearless` file, and the message is `OpenWithOrShortcut` with that path otherwise.
For example:
```
"FearlessCommandQuit"
"FearlessCommandShow hello_world"
"FearlessCommandSelectMains hello_world hello.Hello1 hello.Hello3"
"FearlessCommandRun hello_world Port:51234"
"FearlessCommandRegister Base16:2F646174612F68656C6C6F"
```
(the last one registers `/data/hello` on Linux).
macOS passes an opened file to a manager process as an event, not as an argument.
The launcher script of the 'helper bundle' of the manager (see Extensions on macOS in ReconciliatorDesign) starts the manager with the one argument `FearlessCommandOpened`.
On macOS the one argument `FearlessCommandOpened` leaves no message; on every other system `FearlessCommandOpened` is a bad argument.
The messages of an event are the messages of one argument not starting with `FearlessCommand`, for each path of the event.
On macOS the 'single' manager leaves the messages of every event.
On macOS a manager process that is not the 'single' manager, started with zero arguments or with `FearlessCommandOpened`, waits up to 2 seconds for one event after writing its message files, then leaves the messages of that event.
A manager process that is not the 'single' manager ends after writing its message files; a manager process that becomes the 'single' manager applies its own message files like every other message file.
A manager process started with two or more arguments, or with a bad argument, leaves the message `InvalidCall` with the details. When the 'single' manager handles `InvalidCall`, the 'single' manager shows the error in a dialog and continues running.
A manager process encountering a 'fatal error' while another process holds the lock of `instance.lock` (see The 'single' manager in Implementation notes) leaves the message `FatalError` with the details and ends normally.
The 'single' manager handles `FatalError` as a 'fatal error'.
A manager process encountering a 'fatal error' while no other process holds the lock of `instance.lock` handles that 'fatal error' with the steps at the top of this document.
A manager process encountering a 'fatal error' before that manager process knows the path of its manager folder (the program was not started by the launcher of a manager, or the system does not read file names as UTF-8) only shows the error, then ends: that manager process deletes no folder, leaves no message, and removes no setup.

The 'single' manager runs until `Quit`, `Reset`, a 'fatal error', or the deletion of the manager folder (see I6 in File associations).

## Requests and messages

A request is what one of the verbs below asks the manager to do, whether the request comes from a message or from a GUI operation.
The 'single' manager applies the requests one at a time, in the order they arrive, each one completely before the next.
Applying a `Run` or a `Compile` is starting the mains or the compilation; applying a `Terminate` ends once the JVM of the main has ended.

A message is the file `messages/<time>-<id>.msg` in the manager folder: `<time>` is the 20 digit count of milliseconds since 1970 when the message was written, and `<id>` is a random UUID. The 'single' manager applies the message files in the order of their names, and deletes each message file after reading it.
Only the manager program writes message files: the manager program writes `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp`, then renames `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp` to `messages/<time>-<id>.msg`, so the 'single' manager never reads a partial message. The 'single' manager deletes every `.tmp` file older than 60 seconds.
A manager process whose `.tmp` or `.msg` name already exists picks another `<id>`; the rename never replaces an existing file.
The 'single' manager learns of a new message file from the report of the system that a file appeared in `messages`. When a manager process becomes the 'single' manager, the 'single' manager writes a `.tmp` file in `messages` and waits for the report of that `.tmp` file; no report within 5 seconds is a 'fatal error'.
Every other program commanding the manager (the Eclipse plugin, a script, a test) runs the manager program with a `call` as its argument.
Every GUI operation that is a request has an equivalent message, and every verb is the verb of a GUI operation.
Docs, Base documentation, Browse, Logs and Fearless demos, which open another program on a file or a web page, Info, which opens the Info dialog, the Clear button of an Output, which empties the Output, and Import zip, which writes files and then sends requests, are GUI operations that are not requests and have no message. A GUI operation that finds nothing to open says so in a dialog.
The error of an ill formed message shows in a dialog of the manager window; a hidden manager window is shown first.
Showing the manager window asks the desktop to bring the manager window to the front.

A message file holds a verb, then the arguments of the verb, then the `port` when the message comes from a `call` with a `port`, one per line.
A verb is one of the following, written in upper camel case. Verbs about the whole manager:
- `Show`: show the manager window; with the name of a registered project as its argument, also select that project;
- `Register <path>`: register the folder, or the folder a file is in, then select that project; a folder 'registered already' is only selected;
- `Quit`: end the manager and every main and compilation the manager runs;
- `Reset`: end the manager as a 'fatal error' ends the manager (the steps at the top of this document), with no dialog;
- `ConnectEclipse <path>`: connect the Eclipse found from the path, then say so in a dialog, asking to restart Eclipse if Eclipse is running and to open the Fearless perspective;
- `EditMetadata <path>`: replace `projects.info` with the content of the file at the path (see Registered projects for when the edit is refused);
- `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>`: open the file with the main claiming the extension of the file; when zero or several mains claim that extension, show the conflict dialog (see Opening a file). Double clicking the file on the desktop is the GUI operation of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>`.
- `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`: open the file with the main `<main>` of the project `<name>`, one of the mains claiming the extension of the file. Picking a main in the window that `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>` shows is the GUI operation of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`.
Verbs about one project, taking the name of a registered project:
- `Run <name>`: select the project and run the selected mains of the project, in the order of the selected mains;
- `Compile <name>`: select the project and compile the project if the project is 'out of date'; otherwise the Output says `The project "hello" was already up to date and compiled.`;
- `Check <name>`: select the project and report in the Output of the project the 'problem' of the project, or that the project is 'out of date', or that the project is neither 'invalid' nor 'out of date';
- `Terminate <name>`: select the project and end the main of the project currently running, if any;
- `DeleteCache <name>`: select the project and delete the compilation cache of the project;
- `Kind <name> <kind>`: set the kind of the project to `<kind>`;
- `Forget <name>`: forget the project;
- `Fix <name>`: select the project and fix the project folder (see Fix in FolderShapesDesign);
- `Mount <name> <type> <data>`: set the mount of the DataRead type `<type>` of the project to the data project `<data>` (see DataReadDataEditDesign);
- `Unmount <name> <type>`: remove the mount of the DataRead type `<type>` of the project;
- `Attenuate <name> <main> <attenuator1>..<attenuatorn>`: set the attenuators of the main `<main>` of the project to the attenuators `<attenuator1>..<attenuatorn>`, applied in order; no `<attenuator>` removes all the attenuators of `<main>` (see the Manager section of AttenuationDesign);
- `SelectMains <name> <main1>..<mainn>`: set the selected mains of the project to the mains given; no `<main>` removes the selected mains;
`Forget`, `Kind`, `Mount`, `Unmount`, `Attenuate` and `SelectMains` are special cases of `EditMetadata`: each one is `EditMetadata` with the current `projects.info` changed in one place (the project removed, the `kind` of the project replaced, the mount of one DataRead type replaced or removed, the attenuators of one main replaced, the selected mains of the project replaced).
Such a verb is applied, or is ill formed with the same error, exactly as that `EditMetadata` is; `Mount` is also ill formed when `<data>` is not the name of a registered project.
Before applying the request of a call with a `port`, the manager connects to that port of this machine.
When the connection is made, what the manager sends on the connection starts with a line saying what became of the message: `Applied` once the request is applied, or `Refused` once the message is found ill formed.
After `Refused`, the manager sends the error, as the dialog shows the error, and ends the connection.
After `Applied`, the manager ends the connection at once, except for `Run`, `Compile`, `Check` and `Fix`.
For `Run`, `Compile`, `Check` and `Fix`, after `Applied`, the manager also sends on the connection what the request writes in the Output, from the start of the request and then as the request writes it, with the marks of the Output (see Run, Compile and Check).
The connection then ends when the last main or the compilation ends; when no main and no compilation starts, the connection ends once the text the request writes in the Output is sent.
`Quit` and `Reset` may end the manager before the manager sends `Applied`.
From the start of a `Quit` or a `Reset` until the manager ends, the manager refuses every request the manager reads, with the error `Refused request "Forget": the manager is quitting.` (naming the verb of the request).
Two more kinds of message file, written only by a manager process about a failure of that manager process, hold `InvalidCall` or `FatalError` on the first line and the details on every line after the first line, any number of lines. `InvalidCall` and `FatalError` are not verbs: no `call` and no GUI operation names those messages.

```
call ::= command port?
command ::= FearlessCommandShow projectName?
  | FearlessCommandQuit
  | FearlessCommandReset
  | FearlessCommandRegister base16
  | FearlessCommandConnectEclipse base16
  | FearlessCommandEditMetadata base16
  | FearlessCommandOpenWithOrShortcut base16
  | FearlessCommandOpenWithOrShortcut base16 projectName typeName
  | FearlessCommandRun projectName
  | FearlessCommandCompile projectName
  | FearlessCommandCheck projectName
  | FearlessCommandTerminate projectName
  | FearlessCommandDeleteCache projectName
  | FearlessCommandKind projectName kind
  | FearlessCommandForget projectName
  | FearlessCommandFix projectName
  | FearlessCommandMount projectName typeName projectName
  | FearlessCommandUnmount projectName typeName
  | FearlessCommandAttenuate projectName typeName typeName1..typeNamen
  | FearlessCommandSelectMains projectName typeName1..typeNamen
kind ::= idle | code | dataRead | dataEdit
port ::= Port:1 .. Port:65535
projectName ::= (?!(con|prn|aux|nul|com[1-9]|lpt[1-9])$)[a-z_][a-z0-9_]{0,190}
typeName ::= (?!(con|prn|aux|nul|com[1-9]|lpt[1-9])[.])[a-z][a-z0-9_]*[.]_*[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*'*
```
The `typeName1..typeNamen` of `FearlessCommandSelectMains` are all different.
The `base16` of a `call` names an absolute path that is not the root of a drive.
An argument starting with `FearlessCommand` is a bad argument when the argument is not a `call`, except `FearlessCommandOpened` on macOS (see Starting the 'single' manager).

Ill formed messages:
- a port where no program of this machine accepts the connection;
- a name that is not the name of a registered project; the error lists the registered projects, except for the `<name>` of `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>`, whose error lists the mains claiming the extension of the file (see the `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>` entry below);
- a `Register` whose folder (the path, or the folder holding the file of the path) corresponds to no p with `at(D, p)= id` (see Desks in FolderShapesDesign), or corresponds to a p with `root(D, p)`, with `same(D, p, m)`, `inside(D, p, m)` or `inside(D, m, p)` for m the manager folder, or with `inside(D, p, p2)` or `inside(D, p2, p)` for the project folder p2 of a registered project, or for which the desk refuses a `create` that these `same` and `inside` need;
- a `ConnectEclipse` whose path leads to no Eclipse folder, and an `EditMetadata` whose path names no file;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut` whose path names no file or names an empty file;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>` where `<main>` of `<name>` is not one of the mains claiming the extension of the file, also when `<name>` is not a registered project (the error lists the mains claiming the extension);
- an `EditMetadata` that Registered projects refuses;
- `Run` and `Compile` of a project that is not code, naming the kind of the project;
- `Run` of a project whose `attenuators` name a main that is not a main of the project, or an attenuator that is not an attenuator of the project (the error lists the mains, or the attenuators);
- `Run` of a project with a broken mount, or with a DataRead type with no mount (see DataReadDataEditDesign; the error lists each broken mount, naming what makes that mount broken, and each DataRead type with no mount);
- `Run` and `Compile` of an 'invalid' project (the error is the 'problem' of the project);
- `Run` of a project that is 'out of date', naming that state; `Run` of a project with no selected main, or with a selected main that is not a main of the project (the error lists the mains of the project);
- `Run`, `Compile`, `Check` or `DeleteCache` while a main or a compilation of the project is currently running;
- `Fix` while the project is 'in use' (see Run, Compile and Check);
- `Terminate` while a compilation of the project is currently running;
- an `OpenWithOrShortcut` that runs a main of a project where a `Run` of only that main is ill formed (the error is the error of that `Run`).

Whether a message is ill formed is decided when the 'single' manager starts applying the request of the message, after the 'autoCheck' (see Project states) of the project the request runs on, from the message and the state that 'autoCheck' finds, before anything is compiled, run or written to the Output.
The error of an ill formed message is never in the Output: the Output of a project holds only the results of well formed requests. A failing compilation is the result of a well formed request.

## Info files

The files the manager writes for the manager and for Eclipse (`projects.info`, `manager.info`) are Info files: text in the Fearless character set (the characters a Fearless `Str` allows).
```
info ::= ws (string | list | object) ws
list ::= [[] ws []] | [[] info ([,] info)* []]
object ::= [{] ws [}] | [{] member ([,] member)* [}]
member ::= ws string ws [:] info
string ::= ["](strChar | [\\]["\\n])*["]
ws ::= [ \n]*
```
A `strChar` is a character of the Fearless character set other than `"` and `\`.
The keys of one object are distinct strings.
Inside a string, `\"` is a quote, `\\` a backslash and `\n` a newline.

A path in an Info file is a `taggedText`:
```
taggedText ::= Str:str | UStr:uStr | base16
base16 ::= Base16:([0-9A-F][0-9A-F])+
str ::= strAtom strMore1..strMoren [ ]*
strMore ::= [ ]*[+] strAtom | [ ]*[|^] strAtom?
strAtom ::= [ ]*"[^"\n]*" | [ ]*`[^`\n]*` | [ ]*[(] str [)]
uStr ::= uStrAtom uStrMore1..uStrMoren [ ]*
uStrMore ::= [ ]*[+] uStrAtom | [ ]*[|^] uStrAtom? | [ ]*[.]u codePoints
codePoints ::= [ ]*["]codePoint([ ]codePoint)*["] | [ ]*[`]codePoint([ ]codePoint)*[`]
codePoint ::= [0-9A-F]{1,6}
uStrAtom ::= strAtom [.]u codePoints? | [ ]*[(] uStr [)]
```
Parentheses nest at most 100 deep.
Each `codePoint` is at most `10FFFF` and not from `D800` to `DFFF`.
The text of `Str:str` is the `Str` the Fearless expression `str` evaluates to, and the text of `UStr:uStr` is the `UStr` the Fearless expression `uStr` evaluates to (see `Str` and `UStr` in the standard library: a literal holds its characters as they are, so `\n` inside a literal is two characters).
The text of `base16` is the path whose bytes are the hexadecimal pairs of `base16` (see The bytes of a `Base16:` path in Implementation notes).
For example:
- `"C:\data\hello"` is the path `C:\data\hello`, written in an Info as `"path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\hello\""`;
- `"Hello"|"World"` is `Hello\nWorld`, encoded as `"Str:\"Hello\"|\"World\""`;
- `"caf".u"00E9"` is `caf` followed by the character `00E9`, encoded as `"UStr:\"caf\".u\"00E9\""`.
The manager writes `Str:` then `.escape` of the path when the path is a `Str`; `UStr:` then `.escape` of the path when the path is a `UStr` and not a `Str`; the `base16` of the path when the path is neither a `Str` nor a `UStr`.

## Registered projects

The manager folder holds `projects.info`, the record of the registered projects and of the 'reserve' (see File associations).
The manager is the only program writing `projects.info`: the manager writes the new content to another file in the manager folder, then renames that file to `projects.info`, so a reader such as Eclipse never reads a partial `projects.info`.

`projects.info` is an Info file: an object with `projects`, the list of the registered projects, and `reserve`:
```
{
  "projects": [
    {
      "path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\01_hello_world\"",
      "kind": "code",
      "mains": ["hello.Hello1", "hello.Hello3"],
      "mounts": {"hello.Diary": "diary"},
      "attenuators": {"hello.Hello1": ["base.NoManagerQuit", "hello.NoGui"]}
    },
    {
      "path": "UStr:\"C:\\Users\\Jos\".u\"00E9\"+(\"\\da\".u\"1F600\")+(\"ta\".u)",
      "kind": "dataEdit"
    }
  ],
  "reserve": ["htm"]
}
```
- `reserve`: the extensions of the 'reserve' (see File associations), each one a system extension (see The data in ReconciliatorDesign) without the dot, with 1 to 16 characters, each `a-z` or `0-9`, listed once.
Each project has `path` and `kind`, and may have `mains`, `mounts` and `attenuators`; an absent field is the empty list or the empty object:
- `path`: the absolute path of the project folder, as a `taggedText`; no two projects have `path`s with equal texts (see Info files).
The manager can reach the project folder of the project number i of `projects.info` when the `path` of that project corresponds to a p_i (see Desks in FolderShapesDesign) with `reach(D, i)`, for m the manager folder:
```
reach(D, i)  iff  at(D, p_i)= id, not root(D, p_i), not same(D, p_i, m), not inside(D, p_i, m), not inside(D, m, p_i),
                  forall j < i with reach(D, j): not same(D, p_i, p_j), not inside(D, p_i, p_j), not inside(D, p_j, p_i),
                  and the desk refuses no create(D, p_i s, file) that a same or an inside above with first argument p_i needs
```
- `kind`: `idle`, `code`, `dataRead` or `dataEdit`.
- `mains`: the selected mains, set by `SelectMains`.
- `mounts`: for each DataRead type, the name of the data project mounted on that DataRead type (see DataReadDataEditDesign).
- `attenuators`: for each main the attenuators the manager applies to that main, in order, an attenuator possibly more than once; a main absent from `attenuators` gets no attenuator. Naming a main or an attenuator the project does not have is well formed.

`projects.info` holds no project name (see Registering a folder).
The 'single' manager keeps the names in memory.
A 'name lookup' gives a name to every project of `projects.info`, in the order of `projects.info`: name inference (see Registering a folder) names each project, so no project takes a name of a project before it in `projects.info`.
A 'name check' of a project compares the name of the first marker of the project folder (none when the project folder holds no marker) with the name in memory; when the two names differ, the manager does a 'name lookup'.
The manager does a 'name lookup' at start; before applying a request, the manager does a 'name check' of every project the request names, and a 'name lookup' when the request names a name that is not the name of a project; an 'autoCheck' of a project starts with a 'name check' of the project.
A project folder the manager can not reach keeps the name the manager knew, and gets no name at start; the manager forgets that project (see Project states).

The `mains`, `mounts` and `attenuators` of a project stay when the kind of the project changes, and a mount stays when the data project of the mount is forgotten.
```
A Project hello is a code project with the selected main hello.Hello and the attenuator base.NoManagerQuit on hello.Hello.
B The user makes project hello an idle project.
C The user makes project hello a code project, and compiles project hello.
D Project hello has the selected main hello.Hello, with the attenuator base.NoManagerQuit.

A Project hello is a code project with the mount of the DataRead type hello.Diary to diary. Project diary is a dataEdit project with the folder real_diary.
B The user forgets project diary.
C The user registers the folder test_diary, holding the marker diary.fearless.
D The registered project is named diary, and is idle. The mount of hello.Diary is broken.
E The user makes project diary a dataEdit project.
F The user runs project hello. hello.Hello reads and writes the files of test_diary.
```

A `projects.info` that is not a valid Info file, or does not hold what is above other than the conditions on project folders, is a 'fatal error' pointing at the offending text in the Frontend error message style.

The whole `projects.info` can be edited by hand from the GUI, or via an `EditMetadata` naming a file (outside of the manager folder) with the intended new content.
The editor of the GUI remembers the content the editor opened; when the user commits, and `projects.info` does not hold that content, the editor refuses the commit in a dialog, and the user edits the new content again; otherwise the commit is an `EditMetadata`.
A project of the edit is a project of `projects.info` when the texts of the two `path`s are equal.
An edit is refused, and nothing changes, when:
- the text is not a valid Info file, or does not hold what is above other than the conditions on project folders: the error points at the offending text in the Frontend error message style;
- the edit removes a project, or changes the kind of a project, while that project is 'in use' (see Run, Compile and Check);
- the edit changes the mounts of a project, or gives the name of a data project mounted on a DataRead type of that project to another project folder (through the 'name lookup' after the edit), while a main of that project is currently running;
- the edit removes from `reserve` an extension that a main of a project claims, while a main or a compilation of that project is currently running.
An accepted edit applies at once: projects the edit removes are forgotten, projects the edit adds are registered as they are, the compilation cache (`Coordinator.deleteCache`) of each added project, of each project whose kind changes and of each project whose mains claim an extension the edit removes from `reserve` is deleted, and every project gets an 'autoCheck'.



## Registering a folder

A 'marker' of a folder is a file `<name>.fearless` at the top of that folder, with `<name>` not starting with `.`. The name of a project is the name that name inference gives the project. A project name is a 'valid segment' (see FolderShapesDesign).

A folder p with `same(D, p, p2)` for the project folder p2 of a registered project is 'registered already'.
Registering a folder that is not 'registered already' adds the project at the end of `projects.info`, named by name inference.
Name inference gives the project number i of `projects.info` the name name(i), with m the `<name>` of the first marker of the project folder in the order of the s (see Implementation notes in FolderShapesDesign; no m when the project folder holds no marker), and f the first 64 characters of the 'fixed segment' (see FolderShapesDesign) of the name of the project folder:
```
name(i) = m                                            when ok(i, m)
        = the first n of f, f2, f3, ... with ok(i, n)  otherwise
ok(i, n)  iff  n is a 'valid segment', n != name(j) for every j < i,
               and the project folder holds no file other than the first marker named n.fearless, ignoring case
```
f2, f3, ... are f followed by `2`, `3`, ....
The first marker is renamed (or the marker is created) to match.
If present, the compilation cache (`.fearless_out`) of the newly registered folder is deleted (`Coordinator.deleteCache`).
`.fearless_out` belongs to the Coordinator in every registered folder, whatever the kind of the project.
The newly registered project is in the `idle` state.

Forgetting a project asks no confirmation, and changes nothing in the project folder.

## Project states

Each project is in the first of these states that holds, shown as a badge on the tile of the project:
- `code: busy`: a main or a compilation of the project is currently running;
- `code: invalid content`, `dataRead: invalid content`, `dataEdit: invalid content`: the project folder is not 'well shaped' (see FolderShapesDesign; for code, also the checks of `Coordinator.check`, see CoordinatorDesign), the project folder holds more than one top level `.fearless` file, or the folder `.config/icon` holds more than one PNG file or a PNG file that can not be read;
- `idle`;
- `dataRead`, `dataEdit`;
- `code: compilation cache out of date`: the project is 'out of date';
- `code: compiled`: the project is neither 'invalid' nor 'out of date'.

A project of kind code, dataRead or dataEdit is 'invalid' when a cause of the state `<kind>: invalid content` holds, also in the state `code: busy`. The 'problem' of an 'invalid' project is the error naming which of the causes above holds.
An 'autoCheck' of a project reads the project folder and finds the state of the project and the mains of the project.
An 'autoCheck' that finds the project 'invalid' while no compilation of the project runs writes the 'problem' into `.fearless_out/_error.txt` with `Coordinator.writeError`, for every kind of project.
An 'autoCheck' that can not reach the project folder terminates the main or the compilation of the project, if running, and every running main of a project with a mount of that project, and shows a dialog with the error
`The project "hello" connects to the folder "/home/user/hello".`
`This folder is now unreachable.`
`The project "hello" has now been removed.`
then the 'autoCheck' is a `Forget` of the project.

An 'autoCheck' of a code project checks the marker and the icon of the project, then calls `Coordinator.check` (see CoordinatorDesign), which gives the state of the project: 'invalid' with the 'problem', 'out of date' (`State.outOfDate`), or `State.compiled`.

The mains of a code project are known when `Coordinator.check` gives `State.compiled`: the `.mains` of the `.manifest` of `State.compiled`.

The manager is 'active' while the manager window is shown, and 'passive' while the manager window is hidden (the tray icon or the 'tray icon window' is shown) or minimized.
Showing an error shows the manager window.
An 'active' manager notices changes made on disk by other programs: every three seconds the 'active' manager runs an 'autoCheck' of the selected project and of one other project, taking the other projects in turn. A project gets an 'autoCheck' when a main or a compilation of the project ends.
The 'autoCheck' every three seconds may keep the outcome of the previous 'autoCheck' of the project when every file and every folder of the project folder has a last modification time before the start of that previous 'autoCheck'.
A request (`Check`, `Compile`, `Run`, `Fix`, `OpenWithOrShortcut`) reads the files of the project from scratch, using no outcome of an earlier 'autoCheck'.

The icon of a project is the only PNG file in the folder `.config/icon` of the project, or, with no PNG file in `.config/icon`, an icon generated from the project name.

## General GUI shape
Closing the manager window hides the manager window; an icon stays visible to show that the 'single' manager is still running.
On macOS the icon is the Dock icon of the manager, and the manager shows no tray icon.
Clicking the Dock icon is `Show`.
The Quit of macOS (in the menu of the Dock icon, in the application menu, and Cmd+Q) is `Quit`.
On any other system with a tray (a system tray or StatusNotifierItem), the icon is a tray icon.
Windows always has a tray: the notification area of the taskbar.
Clicking the tray icon, with any button, opens the menu of the tray icon: Show manager (`Show`) and Quit manager (`Quit`).

On a system with no tray, closing the manager window replaces the manager window with the 'tray icon window': a window of the size of a tray icon, with no title bar and no border, holding only the Fearless icon, in the bottom right corner of the screen and always above the other windows.
Clicking the 'tray icon window', with any button, opens the menu of the tray icon.
For half a second after that menu shows, a press or a release on the entries of that menu does nothing.
An entry of that menu is chosen by a click: a press and a release of a button on that same entry.
`Show` removes the 'tray icon window' and shows the manager window.

Dropping files or folders on the manager window is a `Register` of each folder or `.fearless` file dropped, and an `OpenWithOrShortcut` of each other file dropped.
Every button is disabled for half a second after it is pressed.

A menu bar at the top, with the Manager menu: Add folder (`Register`, through a file picker), Edit project metadata (`EditMetadata`), Connect Eclipse (`ConnectEclipse`), Base documentation (`Coordinator.docs` of the package `base`, with any folder), Info (the Info dialog), Reset (`Reset`, after a confirmation dialog saying what Reset does, with the buttons Reset and Cancel, Cancel preselected), Quit (`Quit`); and the Store menu: Fearless demos (opens `https://marcoservetto.github.io/fearless_demos/` with the program that opens web pages), Import zip.
The Info dialog shows the 'drawing line' of this machine (`Coordinator.drawing`, see CoordinatorDesign; the 'drawing line' is defined in the Implementation notes of ImprovedGuiAPI.txt), with the button Close.

Import zip asks, in a dialog, for the URL of a zip, `https:` or `file:`, whose file name is `<name>_app.zip`, `<name>_edu_app.zip`, `<name>_lib.zip` or `<name>_edu_lib.zip`, where `<name>` starts with a lowercase letter and holds only lowercase letters, digits and `_`; a file name ending with `_edu_app.zip` or `_edu_lib.zip` is a `<name>_edu_app.zip` or a `<name>_edu_lib.zip`. The dialog refuses any other URL, shows that the zip is downloading, and shows the error of a download or of a zip that does not hold what follows. `<zip>` is the file name of the zip without `.zip`.
A `<name>_app.zip` or `<name>_edu_app.zip` holds the marker `<name>.fearless` at the top level of the zip. The import writes, with `fearless_imported_zips` next to the manager folder:
```
<name>_app.zip      the folder fearless_imported_zips/<zip>, holding <zip>.zip, and the marker and the PNG files of .config/icon of the zip
<name>_edu_app.zip  the folder fearless_imported_zips/<zip>, holding the files and the folders of the zip
<name>_lib.zip      the file lib/<zip>.zip of the selected project
<name>_edu_lib.zip  the folder lib/<zip> of the selected project, holding the files and the folders of the zip
```
Then the import sends `Register` of `fearless_imported_zips/<zip>`, and `Kind` code and `Compile` of the registered project. With no selected project, the dialog refuses a `<name>_lib.zip` or `<name>_edu_lib.zip`, asking to select a project first.
When the folder or the file the import writes exists already, the import asks whether to replace that folder or that file, and refuses while the project of that folder or that file compiles or runs a main.

The 'desktop with icons' fills the manager window under the menu bar while no project is selected; while a project is selected, the 'desktop with icons' takes the left side of a vertical divider, and the project area takes the right side.
Tiles represent the registered projects, sorted by name: each tile shows the project icon with the state badge of the project, and the project name under the project icon.
A busy project (compiling or running) shows an animated badge.
Clicking a tile is `Show` with the name of the project of the tile.
Double clicking the tile of a project in the state `code: compiled` is `Run` of that project; for a project with one main, the `Run` comes after a `SelectMains` naming that main. For any other project, and for a project with no main, or with several mains and no selected main, a double click is a click.

