## OpenWith and Shortcut

A main 'claims' an extension with `OpenWith` or `Shortcut`: the main asks the manager to start the main on a double click on a file with that extension (see Opening a file). For each `Shortcut` claim, the manager also creates a shortcut file (see Shortcut files).

```
Foo: Main, OpenWith[IconsFoo, "foo"]{ s -> ... }
Bar: Main, Shortcut[IconsBar, "fapp123"]{ s -> ... }
Baz: Main, OpenWith[IconsBaz]{ s -> ... }
Qux: Main, Shortcut[IconsQux]{ s -> ... }
```

`IconsFoo`, `IconsBar`, `IconsBaz` and `IconsQux` are types the compiler generates for image files recognised as autoloaded assets (here `_pkg/icons/foo.png` becomes `IconsFoo`). Those types can come from any package the main can see, including `base`.

### Base

```
OpenWith[I,E]: {}
OpenWith[I]: {}
Shortcut[I,E]: {}
Shortcut[I]: {}
```

A main can implement several of those four types. A type the main implements along several paths is one claim.

### Well formedness

The Frontend checks, before generating code, as well formedness errors:
- a declaration with `OpenWith`, `Shortcut` or `PreLoadIcon` among its supertypes has `Main` among its supertypes;
- the icon is a type name with no generic arguments;
- the extension is a string literal type holding 1 to 16 characters, each `a-z` or `0-9`, other than `fearless`; `"txt"` and `` `txt` `` are the same extension;
- the extension of a `Shortcut` is `fapp` and three digits; the extension of an `OpenWith` is not;
- a main claims each extension at most once.
The Coordinator checks that the icon is the type generated for an image file (see `._frontend` in The Coordinator of CoordinatorDesign).
The exact rules and errors are those of `inject.Methods.checkClaims` of Frontend and of the doc comments of `OpenWith`, `Shortcut` and `PreLoadIcon` in `base`.

### What the manager does after `Coordinator.compile`

Everything below runs in the manager after `Coordinator.compile` ends. When `Coordinator.compile` failed, only step 2 runs. A step that fails makes the compilation fail: the manager calls `Coordinator.writeError` with the error of that step (a refused `.reconcile` does not make the compilation fail, see File associations), and of the steps after that step only step 2 runs.

1. Check that every `Shortcut` claim of `Coordinator.cachedMains` of the project gives a valid shortcut file name (see Shortcut files).
2. Build the in memory map (see The in memory map).
3. Create the missing shortcut files.
4. Call `Coordinator.finish`.

### The in memory map

The manager keeps `extension -> list of (project, main, icon)` in memory only. The manager builds the map at start up, in step 2 of every compilation (see What the manager does after `Coordinator.compile`), after every deletion of the `.fearless_out` of a project, and after every `EditMetadata` that removes a `code` project.
A build reads `Coordinator.cachedMains` of every registered `code` project whose `Coordinator.compile` is not running, and keeps the entries of each project whose `Coordinator.compile` is running.
At start up, before building the map, the manager calls `Coordinator.dropUnfinished` of every registered `code` project.
When a method of `Coordinator` raises or gives `Error.cacheCorrupted` for a project, the manager ends the running main or compilation of that project, if any, calls `Coordinator.deleteCache` of that project, and writes the error with `Coordinator.writeError`.
An `Error.cacheCorrupted` of another project during step 2 of a compilation does not make that compilation fail.
Each operation after which the manager builds the map ends only after the build; the same holds for everything else the manager keeps in memory.

### Autoselected extensions

The Coordinator chooses the extension of a `Shortcut` or an `OpenWith` without extension (see `._autoselect` in The Coordinator of CoordinatorDesign), asking the manager through the `takeFreeExtension` of `Coordinator.compile`.
The manager answers `takeFreeExtension` one call at a time.
The answer is the first extension of the list that no claim in the in memory map uses and that no call answered for a compilation that has not ended yet.

### Shortcut files

For each `Shortcut` claim, the manager creates a file `<name>.<extension>` in the top level of the project, holding a single new line. `<name>` is the simple name of the main turned into a file name, the inverse of the rule autoloading uses to name the type of an image: each uppercase letter becomes `_` and the lowercase of that letter, except the first letter of the name, which only becomes lowercase. `FooBar` becomes `foo_bar`, `_Foo` becomes `_foo`, `Foo_1` becomes `foo_1`, `FooBAR` becomes `foo_b_a_r`.

A main with a `Shortcut` fails the compilation when:
- the simple name of the main has no matching file name: the name ends in primes (`Foo'`), or holds `_` followed by a lowercase letter (`Foo_bar`);
- `<name>` is a 'reserved name' (`Con`, see Names in FolderShapesDesign);
- another main of the project with a `Shortcut` gives the same `<name>` and extension (`a.Bar` and `b.Bar`);
- `<name>` is the name of a top level file without extension of the project (`readme` for `hello.Readme`).
Each case has an error of its own; the error of the first case is
`Main "hello.Foo'" can not have a Shortcut: its name has no matching file name. A main with a Shortcut has a name that can be mapped to a file name like "FooBar" can be mapped to "foo_bar".`

The shortcut file is one of the `.files` of the project (see The data in CoordinatorDesign). The manager never deletes a shortcut file.

### Reconciling

The manager calls `.reconcile` of the Reconciliator (see The data in ReconciliatorDesign):
- after each build of the in memory map;
- in each `EditMetadata` that changes `reserve`, once every other check of the edit (see Registered projects) passes, before the edit applies, with the `reserve` of the edit.
The arguments:
```
identity     the identity of the manager
name         Fearless Manager
command      the launcher of the manager
extensions   each e in captured(M), with icon(e)
taken        empty; T1 gives the taken of the .reconcile after a question
windows      for each icon i of the .preloaded of each main m of Coordinator.cachedMains(p),
             for each registered code project p whose Coordinator.compile is not running:
             .windowClass <desk id of m>-<i.name>, .name the simple name of m, .target p,
             .png the PNG of Coordinator.iconFiles(p, i)
programIcon  the icon of the launcher of the manager
replaced     see File associations
```

#### Desk ids and icons

```
icon(e) = the Fearless icon               when e = .fearless
        = Coordinator.iconFiles(p, i)     when the in memory map gives e exactly one entry (p, m, i)
        = the conflict icon               otherwise
```
The Fearless icon and the conflict icon are files of the `icons` folder of the manager folder (see Conflict icon).
The desk id of a main (the `.deskId` of `Launch`, see The Coordinator in CoordinatorDesign) is `<identity>-<hash>`: the identity of the manager (see The data in ReconciliatorDesign), then the hash of the project folder and the name of the main.
On Windows a running main gives its windows the `.ico` of `Coordinator.iconFiles` directly.

### Opening a file

On `OpenWithOrShortcut <path>` the 'single' manager reacts:

1. One main claims the extension: run that main as `Run` runs a selected main, leaving the selected mains as they are. The running main receives the file as its first input file (see Input files).
2. Zero or several mains claim the extension: the 'conflict dialog' lists those mains with their icons as `project::pkg.Main`, sorted by that text. Closing the conflict dialog runs nothing. `OpenWithOrShortcut <path> <name> <main>` runs `<main>` as in 1.

### Input files

A running main sees its input files through `System.inputCursor` of the standard library: the list of the files given to the program, in the order they arrived.
The list starts with the file opened by `OpenWithOrShortcut`, a document or a shortcut file alike, and starts empty for a main started by `Run`.
Dropping files on a window of a running Fearless program adds those files at the end of the list, in the order of the drop. A drop holding anything that is not a file, a folder for example, adds nothing and is refused.
`System.inputCursor` is a cursor on the first position of the list; `#` gives the file at that position, empty until a file arrives there, and `.next` the cursor on the next position.
An input file is a `mut CapInputCursorNode` of `base`: `.label`, `.readStr`, `.readUStr`, `.readBytes` and `.readImage`. Every read reads the file again, and a file that can not be read any more is a non deterministic error.

### Messages from a running main

A running main sends messages to the manager through `System.manager` of the standard library, the capability `CapManager`.
Each method of `CapManager` sends one message: the method listens on a port, runs the manager program with a `call` ending with that port, and returns when the manager ends the connection.
`.run`, `.compile`, `.check` and `.fix` return the text the manager sent on the port after the line `Applied`; the other methods return `Void`.
`Kind` is `.kindIdle(name)`, `.kindCode(name)`, `.kindDataRead(name)` or `.kindDataEdit(name)`, `Mount` is `.mount(name, type, data)`, `Unmount` is `.unmount(name, type)`, and `Attenuate` is `.attenuate(name, main, attenuators)`.
A path is the list of the bytes naming the path, the bytes of the `Base16:` form of the path. A method whose arguments do not make a `call` (a path that is not absolute or is the root of a drive, a name that is not a `projectName`, a main, a type or an attenuator that is not a `typeName`, `.selectMains` with two equal mains) is a deterministic error of the running main: no message is sent.
A refused message makes the method a deterministic error holding the text after the line `Refused`.
`.quit`, `.reset`, and `.terminate` naming the project of the running main never return: the running main ends.

### Conflict icon

The conflict icon is `base.IconsConflict`, the image `icons/conflict.png` of the folder of `base` (see The data in CoordinatorDesign): the Fearless icon with a purple `?` on top. The manager copies the conflict icon and the Fearless icon, `base.IconsFearless`, into the `icons` folder of the manager folder: the PNG and, on Windows, the `.ico` made from the PNG.

## Future design

What the design will cover later; nothing in this section is current behaviour.
- The message files are replaced by a channel that does not depend on the file system reporting new files, for example a local socket of the 'single' manager.
- The Output keeps only the last part of what a running main or a compilation prints.
- The GUI becomes a separate program: the manager has no window, and the GUI commands the 'single' manager through the manager program with a `call`. The GUI could be a Fearless program with some extra permissions.
- A double click on a file for a main already running adds that file to the input files of the running main, and signals the running main to wake up, for example bringing the window of the running main to the front.
- A 'captured extension' that no main claims gets an icon of its own, showing that the manager holds the extension, instead of the conflict icon.
- TODO: an error written in the Output of a project may need to offer an action that fixes the cause of that error, as the manager asks the user to let the manager hold an extension.
- Removing or changing a data project mounted on a DataRead type of a code project shows a confirmation dialog in the GUI, naming the code projects that get a broken mount.
- A main learns the extensions the main claims, for example through a `System` method.

## Implementation notes

What a rewrite needs that neither this design nor the other repositories say.

An implementation decides alone every detail the design leaves open below the concepts of the design: wire formats, encodings, intervals, the library calls, the content of the files the design names (information for Eclipse can be added to `manager.info`). An implementation never adds what the user or another program can observe and the design does not describe: a file in the manager folder or in a project, a verb, a GUI element, a message, a fallback. Those go into the design first.

- The stand-in for the tray: under Wayland a program can not place the windows of the program, but AWT on Linux is an X11 client (through XWayland under Wayland), so the manager places the window itself.
  The stand-in is an AWT window of type popup. On X11 the window manager does not manage a popup window: no taskbar lists a popup window, and a popup window stays where the manager places the popup window.
- A double click on a button presses the button twice, and the second press comes before the request of the first press changes the window, so the second press sends the same request again.
- A main or a compilation ending alone: the JVM of each main and of each compilation is a process of its own. A process that crashes ends alone. The out of memory killer of the Linux kernel ends one process at a time, chosen by memory use, and Windows ends no group of processes either. systemd-oomd ends a whole cgroup when the memory pressure stays high; a cgroup is a group of processes the Linux kernel accounts together, and a child process starts in the cgroup of the parent process. So on Linux with systemd as init, `Fs.processBuilder` starts each child with `systemd-run --user --scope`, in a transient scope: a cgroup of its own.
  systemd-oomd is on by default on Ubuntu with GNOME, Fedora (GNOME and COSMIC) and Omarchy. systemd-oomd is off on Arch, and missing on Debian, Kubuntu, Lubuntu, Xubuntu and openSUSE Tumbleweed. Void has no systemd.
- A main or a compilation ending with the manager: the manager starts the JVM of each main and of each compilation with a 'parent lifeline' (see Processes in AttenuationDesign) on a pipe the manager holds open.
- Removing a manager folder at once: holding the lock of `instance.lock` of the manager folder, rename the manager folder to a new name in the same folder, then delete the renamed folder. When the rename fails (on Windows a folder holding a file that a process has open may not be renamed), delete every file of the manager folder that can be deleted. A test removes a manager folder whose `instance.lock` is held open, on every desk: the test shows whether the rename works on Windows.
- The 'single' manager: the process holding the lock (`FileChannel.tryLock`) of the file `instance.lock` in the manager folder. The operating system releases the lock when the process ends, however the process ends, so a manager that was killed leaves no stale lock.

- Tray on Linux: `java.awt.SystemTray` needs an XEmbed tray, which GNOME and most Wayland desktops do not have; what those desktops show is a StatusNotifierItem on the session bus, registered with `org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher`, the menu of the item served as `com.canonical.dbusmenu`. A desktop with no owner of the watcher name may still have an XEmbed tray (MATE, i3): there the manager uses `java.awt.SystemTray`, as on Windows. A desktop with neither has no tray. The JDK has no D-Bus client, so the manager speaks the D-Bus wire protocol itself on the `unix:path=` socket of `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`.
- Clicks on the tray icon on Linux: each desktop decides what a click on a StatusNotifierItem does.
  Some desktops show the menu served as `com.canonical.dbusmenu` themselves.
  Other desktops report the click to the item: `Activate` (usually a left click), `SecondaryActivate` (usually a middle click) or `ContextMenu` (usually a right click), each with the point of the click.
  The manager answers each of these three by opening the menu of the tray icon at that point, with the same entries.
  So the click opening the menu may differ from one desktop to another (left or right, single or double), and the menu may look different for different clicks on the same desktop; every one of those clicks opens the menu.
- Some file systems, for example some network drives, do not report new files.
- A desktop that keeps windows from taking the focus, such as GNOME, may leave the manager window behind the window the user is working in; such a desktop tells the user in its own way, for example with a notification that Fearless Manager is ready.
- The bytes of a `Base16:` path: on Windows the UTF-16 code units of the name, little endian (a name may hold an unpaired surrogate); elsewhere the bytes `sun.jnu.encoding` gives, the bytes the JVM turns names into.
- Port of a message: a TCP port on the loopback address. The manager connects, sends the status line, then the reason of a refusal or the Output of the mains or of the compilation, as UTF-8 text, then closes the connection. Eclipse and a running main listen on a port the system picks.
- Registering a folder whose marker has the right name and is empty writes a newline into the marker, so the marker holds what a created marker holds: GNOME types every empty file as `application/x-zerosize`, whatever the name of the file, and never hands an empty file to the manager.
- `base` autoloads the assets of `base` as if the files of `base` were under a `_base` folder: the `.path` of `base.IconsConflict` is `fear:/_base/icons/conflict.png` and the `.diskPath` is `icons/conflict.png`, relative to the folder of `base`, which a running main receives as the JVM property `fearlessBase.dir`.
- A claim whose icon file can not be read shows, in the manager window, the icon generated from `project::pkg.Main`, as the icon of a project is generated from the project name.
- The last premise of `ok(i, n)` in name inference: on a file system ignoring case, that other file and the file `n.fearless` are one file.
